Hand–Forearm Perceptual Module with Distributed Perception

ouadi Maakoul 

AI: chatgpt, Claude, Deepseek, Meta AI


Abstract

Robotic manipulation under external occlusion is fundamentally limited by the spatial concentration of perception. When the line of sight between an external camera and the contact region is lost, the contact state, local object geometry, and friction parameters become unobservable from proprioception and wrist force/torque alone. This work formalizes a hand–forearm perceptual module in which the forearm functions simultaneously as actuator, tendon transmission, and internal perceptual organ, and in which the wrist state acts as an explicit separator between hand-side and forearm-side inference. The central theoretical contribution is a rigorous observability analysis demonstrating that under external occlusion, the distributed architecture possesses a strictly larger observable subspace than a centralized architecture with equivalent sensor count.

The state space is defined with per-contact dimension 8, eliminating the friction double-counting that arises when μ is treated as a per-contact rather than per-material-pair quantity. The rigid-body dynamics are formulated as an inclusion accounting for the underactuation of tendon-driven hands, with the actuation-unreachable subspace (cokernel of the tendon-to-joint transmission matrix) explicitly characterized. The probabilistic graphical model establishes the wrist pose x_wrist ∈ SE(3) as a separator between hand and forearm states, while the wrist force/torque measurement acts as a coupling factor that introduces a non-zero off-diagonal block in the information matrix. Observability is established as a strict rank-increase result under explicit transversality and persistent-excitation assumptions, with a tight lower bound of 8k − 6 + m_int for k contacts and m_int independently resolvable internal tendon modes.

Identifiability of contact parameters is grounded in the constant-rank/immersion theorem, with friction analysis distinguishing the Coulomb inequality from the slip-boundary equality and explicitly limiting the scope to single-material-pair objects. The internal perception model incorporates retro-reflective tendon markers with staggered depth placement and acknowledges the practical rank limitation m_int ≤ 3 for a bundle view. The information-theoretic analysis is recast as a rate-distortion statement: positive mutual information gain implies strictly lower conditional entropy, but quantitative MSE reduction must be measured empirically rather than derived.

The technical specification is made self-consistent: m = 6 tendons and 8 motors (including 2 direct-drive wrist motors), with an actuation-unreachable subspace dimension of 9, GMSL2 aggregation through MAX96724 deserializers for 8 cameras, a power budget of 78–98 W continuous with mandatory active cooling, and a 38 ms latency budget compatible with 50 ms slip detection. The document concludes with falsifiable experimental predictions and a protocol for isolating the architectural effect from sensor-level effects.

