1. Introduction

Robotic manipulation in unstructured environments requires the estimation of contact state, local object geometry, and friction parameters from noisy, partial measurements. When an external camera maintains line of sight, visual perception provides dense spatial information about the object and the hand. When occlusion occurs — as it inevitably does during precision grasping, in-hand manipulation, and insertion — the external visual channel becomes uninformative, and the estimation problem reduces to whatever information can be extracted from proprioception and wrist force/torque sensing.

This work argues that the spatial distribution of heterogeneous sensors along the hand–forearm mechanical chain is not merely an engineering convenience but a structural determinant of observability. The forearm is treated as a perceptual organ in its own right, housing tendon tension sensors, motor current sensors, and an internal camera observing the tendon transmission. The wrist is treated as an explicit fusion node separating arm-side and hand-side inference. The central theoretical claim is that under external occlusion, the distributed architecture observes a strictly larger subspace of the state space than a centralized architecture with equivalent sensor count.

The document is organized as follows. Section 2 defines the state space and dynamics with full dimensional consistency. Section 3 develops the measurement model and derives sensor ranks. Section 4 formulates the probabilistic graphical model with the corrected separator structure. Section 5 presents the observability theory with explicit assumptions and proofs. Section 6 addresses identifiability of contact parameters. Section 7 develops the internal perception model. Section 8 provides the information-theoretic analysis. Section 9 specifies the architecture and design. Section 10 provides the technical specification. Section 11 states experimental predictions. Section 12 concludes.

