3. Measurement Model and Sensor Characterization

3.1 General Measurement Model

Each sensor i ∈ {1, …, N} produces a measurement

z_i = h_i(x) + v_i, v_i ∼ N(0, R_i(t, T)),

where h_i: X → ℝ^{d_i} is the observation model and R_i(t, T) ≻ 0 is a time- and temperature-dependent noise covariance modeled as

R_i(t, T) = R_i^{(0)} + Σ_wear(n_g) + Σ_thermal(T),

with n_g the cumulative grasp count. The sensors partition into four families:

S = S_prop ∪ S_tact ∪ S_vis ∪ S_int,

where S_prop denotes proprioception (encoders, IMU), S_tact denotes tactile sensing, S_vis denotes visual sensing, and S_int denotes internal mechanical state sensing.

3.2 GelSight Model

GelSight uses photometric stereo to reconstruct a depth map z_GS: Ω → ℝ of the elastomer surface, where Ω ⊂ ℝ² is the image domain. The depth map relates to the contact state through

z_GS(u, v) = η_GS(p, n, f_n, G_object) + v_GS,

where G_object is the local object geometry. The contact position p is recovered from the centroid of the depth map, the normal n from the gradient, and the normal force f_n from the integral. This gives

rank(∂z_GS/∂(p, n, f_n)) = 3 + 2 + 1 = 6

conditional on G_object being known. If G_object is unknown, the joint Jacobian

∂z_GS/∂(p, n, f_n, G_object)

is not full rank, and the geometry must be resolved through the palm cameras or the visual channel.

3.3 TacTip Model

TacTip tracks marker displacements {d_j}_{j=1}^{N_m} within a soft optical dome. The marker displacements relate to the contact state through

d_j = η_{TT,j}(p, n, f_t) + v_{TT,j}.

The tangential force f_t lies in the tangent plane T_{n}S² ≅ ℝ², so dim f_t = 2. The friction coefficient μ_o is a world-state parameter, not a contact-state variable. The rank contribution to the contact state is therefore

rank(∂d/∂(f_t)) = 2.

The friction coefficient μ_o is identifiable through incipient slip detection, as detailed in Section 6.1.

3.4 Combined Tactile Rank

GelSight provides rank 6 in (p, n, f_n) conditional on G_object. TacTip provides rank 2 in f_t. The combined per-contact rank is

rank(∂(z_GS, z_TT)/∂(p, n, f)) = 8

when G_object is known.

3.5 F/T Model

The wrist F/T sensor measures the total wrench:

z_FT = Σ_{i=1}^k [ f_i ; (p_i − p_sensor) × f_i ] + v_FT ∈ ℝ⁶,

where the moment arm is taken relative to the sensor frame.

