6. Identifiability of Contact Parameters

Definition 6.1. The contact state c = (p, n, f) is identifiable from measurements (z_T, z_V, z_Q) if the map

(c, w) ↦ (z_T, z_V, z_Q)

is injective on the domain of interest.

Theorem 6.2 (Sufficient conditions for identifiability). The contact state c = (p, n, f) is locally identifiable if:

1. the tactile sensor resolves the pressure map z_T at the contact patch with sufficient spatial resolution;
2. the visual system observes the contact from at least two non-collinear viewpoints;
3. the tactile sensor is sensitive to shear, so that f_t is observable;
4. the object geometry G_object is either known or independently constrained by the palm cameras.

Proof. Conditions 1–3 ensure that the map (c, w) ↦ (z_T, z_V) has full column rank at the contact configuration. By the constant-rank/immersion theorem, full column rank of the Jacobian implies local injectivity (local immersion). Condition 4 ensures that the unknown geometry does not leave the joint Jacobian rank-deficient. ∎

Corollary 6.3. Under the conditions of Theorem 6.2, the Jacobian of the map (c, w) ↦ (z_T, z_V, z_Q) has full column rank on an open set of contact configurations.

6.1 Friction Identifiability

The Coulomb friction condition is

‖f_t‖ ≤ μ f_n

in the non-slipping case, with equality at the slip boundary. In a stable, non-slipping grasp, μ is not identifiable from a single static observation. The friction coefficient is identifiable under:

1. Slip or incipient slip. At the slip boundary, ‖f_t‖ = μ f_n, and μ is recoverable from the ratio ‖f_t‖/f_n. TacTip detects incipient slip through lateral marker displacement.
2. Active excitation. By varying f_n and observing slip onset, the friction cone boundary is mapped.
3. Shear sensing. A shear-sensitive tactile sensor measures the tangential displacement field, from which f_t and μ are inferred under a known contact model.

The heterogeneous configuration — GelSight for geometry and normal force, TacTip for shear and incipient slip — makes μ identifiable under incipient slip or active excitation.

