7. Internal Perception and Tendon State Estimation

7.1 Tendon Elasticity Model

The tendon force–length relationship is modeled as

τ(ΔL, L̇) = τ_active(u) + τ_passive(ΔL) + τ_damping(L̇),

with

τ_passive(ΔL) = k_tendon ΔL if ΔL > 0, 0 if ΔL ≤ 0.

7.2 Bowden-Cable Friction with Backlash and Elongation

The capstan equation is

τ_out = τ_in exp(−μ_c ∫_0^s κ(s') ds').

For direction-dependent transmission with backlash and elongation, the model is

τ_joint(t) = τ_in e^{−μ_c θ(q)} − B_c − (L/EA) τ_avg if q̇ > 0,
τ_joint(t) = τ_in e^{+μ_c θ(q)} + B_c − (L/EA) τ_avg if q̇ < 0,

where B_c > 0 is the backlash dead-zone magnitude, L is the cable length, E is the Young's modulus, A is the cross-sectional area, and τ_avg is the average tension. The inverse map from ΔL to Δq requires compensation for B_c and the elongation term. In the dead zone, the inverse is ill-posed, reducing the effective m_int to less than 3 near zero velocity.

7.3 Internal Perception with Retro-Reflective Markers

The internal perception map is

δ = Ψ(τ, q, L), z_P = Φ(δ) + v_P,

with linearization

z_P ≈ Φ(δ_0) + J_Φ J_Ψ [Δτ; Δq; ΔL] + v_P.

A 0.4 mm Dyneema tendon is not visible to a standard camera at 220 mm distance under normal illumination. Retro-reflective markers are placed on each tendon termination spring, making each tendon individually resolvable. With m = 6 markers visible, the rank is

rank(J_Φ J_Ψ|_L) ≤ 6,

but the practical rank is limited by occlusion and perspective. For the bundle geometry, m_int ≤ 3 independently resolvable modes is realistic, provided markers are staggered in depth by at least 5 mm to overcome the aperture problem.

Proposition 7.1. If rank(J_Φ J_Ψ) ≥ m_int, then the internal camera observation z_P provides an independent constraint on ΔL that is not available from motor current and encoders alone.

Proof. Motor current provides a noisy estimate of τ_in, and encoders provide q. The map from (τ_in, q) to ΔL requires the friction model of Section 7.2, which is uncertain. The internal camera observes δ, which depends on (Δτ, Δq, ΔL). If the Jacobian has rank at least m_int, the map is locally invertible in ΔL, providing a direct measurement that bypasses the friction uncertainty. ∎

