8. Information-Theoretic Analysis

8.1 Probabilistic Grounding

Let p(w) be a prior over world states specified by the task distribution, and let p(z | w, q, τ) be the measurement likelihood. The mutual information I(w; z | q, τ) is defined with respect to this probability space.

The local world state is defined as

w_local = (G_object|_{B(p, ρ)}, μ_o),

where B(p, ρ) is the ball of radius ρ centered at the contact point p, with ρ ≈ 10 mm equal to the tactile patch radius.

8.2 Positive Information under Occlusion

Theorem 8.1. Under M = 0, the distributed architecture provides strictly positive information about the local object state:

I(w_local; S_tact, S_vis, palm | M = 0, q, τ) > 0.

Proof. The tactile measurement z_T = h_T(c, w_local) + v_T depends on w_local through the contact geometry and local object surface. Since the tactile sensor is in physical contact with the object, I(w_local; z_T | M = 0, q, τ) > 0 whenever the contact patch contains local geometric information and R_T ≻ 0. The palm cameras observe the inter-digital space. Therefore I(w_local; S_tact, S_vis, palm | M = 0) ≥ I(w_local; z_T | M = 0) > 0. ∎

8.3 Rate-Distortion Statement

Definition 8.2. The architectural information gain under occlusion is

ΔI_occ = I(w_local; S_dist | M = 0) − I(w_local; S_ext | M = 0).

Corollary 8.3. ΔI_occ > 0 and is bounded below by

ΔI_occ ≥ I(w_local; z_T | M = 0) > 0.

Proposition 8.4. The information gain implies strictly lower conditional entropy:

ΔI_occ > 0 ⇒ h(w_local | S_dist, M = 0) < h(w_local | S_ext, M = 0).

For any distortion D, the rate-distortion functions satisfy

R_dist(D) ≤ R_ext(D) − ΔI_occ.

The quantitative reduction in MSE must be measured empirically. Mutual information gives a lower bound on Bayes risk, not an upper bound.

Proof. By definition,

I(w; S_dist | M = 0) = h(w | M = 0) − h(w | S_dist, M = 0),
I(w; S_ext | M = 0) = h(w | M = 0) − h(w | S_ext, M = 0).

Subtracting gives the claim. The rate-distortion inequality follows from the data-processing inequality. ∎

