9. Architecture and Design

9.1 Tendon and Motor Configuration

The hand uses m = 6 tendons:

· Thumb: 2 tendons (flexor and extensor).
· Index, middle, ring, little: 1 tendon each (flexor), with a shared return spring providing extension.

The motors are:

· Wrist: 2 direct-drive motors.
· Thumb: 2 motors (one per tendon).
· Index, middle, ring, little: 1 motor each (flexor tendon).

Total motors: 2 + 2 + 4 = 8.

The actuation-unreachable subspace dimension is

dim U(q) = n − rank(B_total) = 17 − 8 = 9.

The 9-dimensional actuation-unreachable subspace is distributed across the fingers and is resolved by compliance, contact forces, and the internal perception channel. The internal camera reduces the effective unobservable subspace by at most m_int ≤ 3 through direct tendon-length observation.

9.2 Three-Level Architecture

Level C — Shoulder (standardized interface). ISO 9409-1-50 flange, 48 V power, EtherCAT. Mounted on UR10e, Franka, or Kinova.

Level B — Forearm (perceptual mechatronic core). Length 220–240 mm. 8 brushless motors (Faulhaber 1226 class, continuous power 9.9 W each). Tendon transmission: Dyneema 0.4 mm, PTFE tubes, idler pulleys. Internal sensors: cable tension cells, motor current, temperature, 512 imp/rev encoders, 6-axis IMU. Internal wide-angle camera observing retro-reflective markers on tendon terminations. Computing: 1× Orin Nano + Artix-7 XC7A100T FPGA.

Level A — Hand (active perceptual network).

· Wrist (fusion node): 2 DoF, 6-axis F/T sensor, 120° RGB camera + miniature ToF near palm.
· Palm (anti-occlusion): 2 micro-cameras viewing between fingers, 1 central GelSight Svelte or DIGIT.
· Fingers: per finger, 1 tendon + 1 return spring. Modalities: joint pose from internal marker tracking, 1D/3D force sensor at pulp, TacTip or GelSight360 at distal pulp, lateral micro-camera in V0.1b.

