12. Conclusion

This work has formalized a hand–forearm perceptual module with distributed perception, established a rigorous observability theory showing strict rank increase under occlusion, provided an information-theoretic quantification of the occlusion gap, and derived identifiability conditions for contact parameters.

The central claim is that the spatial distribution of heterogeneous sensors along the hand–forearm mechanical chain is a structural determinant of observability. Under occlusion, the distributed architecture observes a strictly larger subspace of the state space than a centralized architecture with equivalent sensor count. This is a mathematical property of the sensing architecture, not merely an engineering convenience.

The falsifiable predictions are: strict rank increase under occlusion with lower bound 8k − 6 + m_int, positive information gain ΔI_occ > 0, and largest rank deficiency upon ablation of tactile sensing. These predictions are testable with the experimental protocol described, and the results will determine whether the theoretical framework is confirmed or falsified.


Appendix A: Detailed Proofs and Mathematical Derivations

A.1 Dimensional Consistency of the State Space

The full state space is defined as

\mathcal{X} = \mathcal{Q} \times T\mathcal{Q} \times \mathcal{L} \times \mathcal{T} \times \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{W},

with \mathcal{Q} \subset \mathbb{R}^n, n = 17; \mathcal{L}, \mathcal{T} \subset \mathbb{R}^m, m = 6; and \mathcal{C} the contact manifold. The dimension is

\dim \mathcal{X} = 2n + 2m + 8k + d_w = 46 + 8k + d_w.

Under the quasi-static reduction, the velocity subspace T\mathcal{Q} is removed:

\dim \mathcal{X}_{\text{qs}} = n + 2m + 8k + d_w = 29 + 8k + d_w.

The contact state per contact is c_i = (p_i, n_i, f_i) \in \mathbb{R}^3 \times S^2 \times \mathbb{R}^3, with \dim c_i = 3 + 2 + 3 = 8. The friction coefficient \mu_o is a material-pair property belonging to w, not to c_i. This eliminates the double-counting that arises when \mu is treated as a per-contact variable.

A.2 Actuation-Unreachable Subspace

The total actuation map is

B_{\text{total}}(q) = [B(q) \;\; B_{\text{wrist}}] \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times (m+2)} = \mathbb{R}^{17 \times 8},

where B(q) \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times m} is the tendon-to-joint transmission matrix and B_{\text{wrist}} \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times 2} is the direct-drive wrist actuation matrix. The actuation-unreachable subspace is

\mathcal{U}(q) = (\operatorname{Im} B_{\text{total}}(q))^\perp = \ker B_{\text{total}}(q)^T \subset \mathbb{R}^n.

For generic configurations where B_{\text{total}} has full column rank 8,

\dim \mathcal{U}(q) = 17 - 8 = 9.

The term "null space" is reserved for \ker B_{\text{total}}(q) \subset \mathbb{R}^{m+2}, which is generically \{0\} when B_{\text{total}} has full column rank. The quasi-static equilibrium inclusion is

g(q) + \sum_{i=1}^{k} J_i(q)^T f_i \in \operatorname{Im} B_{\text{total}}(q) + \mathcal{K}(q),

where the compliance-induced wrench subspace is

\mathcal{K}(q) = \{ K_q \Delta q + B(q) K_t \Delta L : \Delta q \in \mathbb{R}^n, \Delta L \in \mathbb{R}^m \}.

The compliance-weighted pseudoinverse for the joint error is

\Delta q_{\text{compliance}} = (K_q + B K_t B^T)^{-1} B K_t \Delta L.

The minimum-norm pseudoinverse B^\dagger corresponds to the limit K_q \to 0, which is not physical.

A.3 Observability Rank Increase

Lemma A.1 (External architecture under occlusion)

Under Assumptions 5.1–5.2 and quasi-static reduction on \mathcal{X}_{\text{qs}},

\operatorname{rank}(H_{\text{ext}}) \leq n + 6 = 23.

The instantaneous unobservable subspace has dimension at least

\dim \mathcal{X}_{\text{qs}} - (n + 6) = (29 + 8k + d_w) - 23 = 6 + 8k + d_w.

Proof. The external architecture has h_{\text{ext}} = (h_{\text{prop}}, h_{\text{FT}}, h_{\text{vis, ext}}). Under occlusion, h_{\text{vis, ext}} contributes zero rank. Proprioception contributes n rows under quasi-static reduction. The F/T sensor contributes at most 6 rows. ∎

Lemma A.2 (Tactile rank increase)

Under Assumptions 5.3–5.4, the tactile measurement adds exactly

8k - 6

independent rows beyond proprioception and F/T.

Proof. The tactile Jacobian H_T \in \mathbb{R}^{8k \times 8k} has full rank 8k by Assumption 5.3. The F/T Jacobian H_{\text{FT}} \in \mathbb{R}^{6 \times 8k} has full row rank 6 by Assumption 5.4. The row space of H_{\text{FT}} is contained in the row space of H_T, with intersection dimension exactly 6. Therefore the number of independent rows contributed by tactile beyond F/T is exactly 8k - 6. ∎

Lemma A.3 (Internal perception rank increase)

Under Assumption 5.5, the internal camera adds at least m_{\text{int}} independent rows beyond proprioception, F/T, and tactile.

Proposition A.4 (Instantaneous rank increase under occlusion)

Under Assumptions 5.1–5.5, at a state x with M_{\text{vis}} = 0 and k \geq 2 contacts,

\operatorname{rank}(H_{\text{dist}}) - \operatorname{rank}(H_{\text{ext}}) \geq (8k - 6) + m_{\text{int}}.

For k = 2 and m_{\text{int}} = 3, the lower bound is 16 - 6 + 3 = 13.

Proof. By Lemma A.1, \operatorname{rank}(H_{\text{ext}}) \leq n + 6. By Lemmas A.2 and A.3, the distributed architecture adds at least (8k - 6) + m_{\text{int}} independent rows. ∎

Proposition A.5 (Windowed rank increase)

Under Assumptions 5.1–5.6, there exists an open set \mathcal{X}_{\text{occ}} \subset \mathcal{X}_{\text{qs}} such that for all x \in \mathcal{X}_{\text{occ}},

\operatorname{rank}(\mathcal{O}_{\text{dist}}(x)) > \operatorname{rank}(\mathcal{O}_{\text{ext}}(x)).

Proof. By Proposition A.4, the instantaneous rank increase is at least (8k - 6) + m_{\text{int}}. Under Assumption 5.6, the null space of H_t is not invariant under the dynamics, so the propagated rows preserve this rank increase over the window. ∎

A.4 Identifiability and the Immersion Theorem

Theorem 6.2 uses the constant-rank/immersion theorem, not the inverse function theorem. The Jacobian

\frac{\partial (z_T, z_V)}{\partial (c, w)} \in \mathbb{R}^{(d_T + d_V) \times (8k + d_w)}

maps a lower-dimensional domain to a higher-dimensional codomain. Full column rank implies local injectivity (local immersion). The inverse function theorem requires a square, invertible Jacobian and is not applicable here.

A.5 Friction Identifiability

The Coulomb friction condition is

\|f_t\| \leq \mu f_n

in the non-slipping case, with equality at the slip boundary. In a stable, non-slipping grasp, \mu is not identifiable from a single static observation. The friction coefficient is identifiable under:

1. Slip or incipient slip. At the slip boundary, \|f_t\| = \mu f_n, and \mu is recoverable from the ratio \|f_t\| / f_n. TacTip detects incipient slip through lateral marker displacement.
2. Active excitation. By varying f_n and observing slip onset, the friction cone boundary is mapped.
3. Shear sensing. A shear-sensitive tactile sensor measures the tangential displacement field, from which f_t and \mu are inferred under a known contact model.

Scope assumption. The world state w includes a single material-pair friction coefficient \mu_o. This restricts the analysis to objects with homogeneous surface material.

A.6 Internal Perception Rank Bound

The internal perception map is

\delta = \Psi(\tau, q, L), \qquad z_P = \Phi(\delta) + v_P,

with linearization

z_P \approx \Phi(\delta_0) + J_\Phi J_\Psi \begin{bmatrix} \Delta \tau \\ \Delta q \\ \Delta L \end{bmatrix} + v_P.

A 0.4 mm Dyneema tendon at 220 mm distance with a 120° lens gives a pixel size of approximately 0.3 mm at the termination plane. Retro-reflective markers must be greater than 2 mm to be resolved. With m = 6 markers visible, the rank is bounded by

\operatorname{rank}(J_\Phi J_\Psi|_L) \leq 6,

but the practical rank is limited by occlusion and perspective. The depth along the bundle axis is not observable from a single camera (aperture problem). Staggering markers in z by at least 5 mm allows m_{\text{int}} \leq 3 independently resolvable modes.

A.7 Information-Theoretic Analysis

The local world state is

w_{\text{local}} = (G_{\text{object}}|_{B(p, \rho)}, \mu_o),

where B(p, \rho) is the ball of radius \rho \approx 10 mm centered at the contact point p. The architectural information gain is

\Delta I_{\text{occ}} = I(w_{\text{local}}; S_{\text{dist}} \mid M = 0) - I(w_{\text{local}}; S_{\text{ext}} \mid M = 0).

Corollary A.6. \Delta I_{\text{occ}} > 0 and is bounded below by

\Delta I_{\text{occ}} \geq I(w_{\text{local}}; z_T \mid M = 0) > 0.

Proposition A.7. The information gain implies strictly lower conditional entropy:

\Delta I_{\text{occ}} > 0 \implies h(w_{\text{local}} \mid S_{\text{dist}}, M=0) < h(w_{\text{local}} \mid S_{\text{ext}}, M=0).

For any distortion D, the rate-distortion functions satisfy

R_{\text{dist}}(D) \leq R_{\text{ext}}(D) - \Delta I_{\text{occ}}.

Proof. By definition,

I(w; S_{\text{dist}} \mid M=0) = h(w \mid M=0) - h(w \mid S_{\text{dist}}, M=0),

I(w; S_{\text{ext}} \mid M=0) = h(w \mid M=0) - h(w \mid S_{\text{ext}}, M=0).

Subtracting gives the claim. The rate-distortion inequality follows from the data-processing inequality. ∎

