Appendix C: Tactile Sensor Models

C.1 GelSight Photometric Stereo

GelSight employs photometric stereo with RGB LEDs to reconstruct a depth map z_{\text{GS}}: \Omega \to \mathbb{R} of the elastomer surface. The depth map is inferred by integrating estimated surface gradients, which can accumulate reconstruction errors. Calibration uses a ball with known radius (around 2–3 mm) pressed over the surface.

The depth map relates to the contact state through

z_{\text{GS}}(u, v) = \eta_{\text{GS}}(p, n, f_n, G_{\text{object}}) + v_{\text{GS}}.

The rank conditional on G_{\text{object}} being known is

\operatorname{rank}\left(\frac{\partial z_{\text{GS}}}{\partial (p, n, f_n)}\right) = 3 + 2 + 1 = 6.

C.2 TacTip Incipient Slip Detection

TacTip tracks marker displacements \{d_j\}_{j=1}^{N_m} within a soft optical dome. The addition of ridges—mimicking friction ridges in the human fingertip—in a concentric ring pattern allows for localised shear and incipient slip detection. Incipient slip is defined as the moment the central external marker and the ArUco marker start moving downwards, with no relative movement between them. The rank contribution to the contact state is

\operatorname{rank}(\partial d / \partial f_t) = 2.

