Appendix D: Tendon Transmission and Friction

D.1 Dyneema Tendon Properties

Dyneema is a durable synthetic fiber used in tendon-driven robot hands such as the DLR hand. The DLR hand achieves a payload of 20 N active fingertip force with steel tendons and 40 N with Dyneema. Braided high-test Dyneema wire of 0.5 mm diameter has been used in soft robotic gloves.

D.2 Bowden-Cable Friction with Backlash

The friction between the tendon and sheath causes hysteresis, backlash, and direction-dependent behavior. Nonlinear friction inevitably induces tendon elongation and backlash-like hysteresis. The model is

\tau_{\text{joint}}(t) =
\begin{cases}
\tau_{\text{in}} e^{-\mu_c \theta(q)} - B_c - \frac{L}{EA} \tau_{\text{avg}}, & \dot{q} > 0, \\[4pt]
\tau_{\text{in}} e^{+\mu_c \theta(q)} + B_c - \frac{L}{EA} \tau_{\text{avg}}, & \dot{q} < 0,
\end{cases}

where B_c > 0 is the backlash dead-zone magnitude, L is the cable length, E is the Young's modulus, A is the cross-sectional area, and \tau_{\text{avg}} is the average tension. In the dead zone, the inverse from \Delta L to q is ill-posed, reducing the effective m_{\text{int}} to less than 3 near zero velocity.

